[2026-03-14T19:06:48.202Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MP5AXfSww289mGycsaAzYV","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# app-building\n     2→\n     3→Simple and extensible platform for dark factory agentic app building: creating apps\n     4→according to a spec without human involvement along the way. Example use cases:\n     5→\n     6→* `npm run agent -- --branch feature/my-app -p \"Build me an app XYZ based on this spec: ...\"`\n     7→* `npm run agent -- --branch feature/my-app -p \"Continue maintaining app XYZ and fix these bugs: ...\"`\n     8→* `npm run agent -- --branch feature/my-app -i` for interactive access to the agent.\n     9→\n    10→### Tech stack\n    11→\n    12→Every app the agent builds uses the same stack:\n    13→\n    14→* **React** — frontend UI\n    15→* **Netlify** — hosting and serverless functions (backend API)\n    16→* **Neon** — serverless Postgres database\n    17→* **Playwright** — integration tests\n    18→\n    19→If you need a different stack, this platform isn't the right fit yet.\n    20→\n    21→Core ideas:\n    22→\n    23→* The agent runs within a docker container that clones a repo and exposes an HTTP server for control.\n    24→* The agent builds by following a set of skill documents with guides\n    25→  for breaking its work down into tasks and directives for performing those tasks.\n    26→* The agent commits logs for it to review later and improve its skills.\n    27→* All code changes are committed and pushed back to the remote from inside the container.\n    28→\n    29→Containers can run locally or remotely.\n    30→\n    31→## Prerequisites\n    32→\n    33→- **Node.js 18+**\n    34→- **A GitHub repo** to build into, with a feature branch checked out (not `main`)\n    35→\n    36→## Setup\n    37→\n    38→```bash\n    39→npm install\n    40→```\n    41→\n    42→Copy `.env.example` to `.env` and fill in the values:\n    43→\n    44→| Variable | Required | Description |\n    45→|---|---|---|\n    46→| `INFISICAL_CLIENT_ID` | Yes | Infisical machine identity Client ID |\n    47→| `INFISICAL_CLIENT_SECRET` | Yes | Infisical machine identity Client Secret |\n    48→| `INFISICAL_PROJECT_ID` | Yes | Infisical project ID |\n    49→| `INFISICAL_ENVIRONMENT` | Yes | Infisical environment (e.g. `prod`) |\n    50→| `FLY_API_TOKEN` | For remote | Fly.io personal access token |\n    51→| `FLY_APP_NAME` | For remote | Fly.io app name for container namespace |\n    52→| `LOCAL_CONTAINER_PORT` | No | Override the host port for local containers (auto-selected if unset) |\n    53→\n    54→Build secrets (API keys for Anthropic, GitHub, Netlify, Neon, etc.) are managed in **Infisical** under the `/global/` folder. The orchestration scripts fetch them at container startup and inject them into the container environment. See `src/package/.env.example` for the full list of required build secrets.\n    55→\n    56→Per-branch deployment secrets (database URLs, site IDs) are stored in Infisical under `/branches/<branch>/` and fetched by the container at deploy time.\n    57→\n    58→### Infisical setup\n    59→\n    60→1. Create a project at [app.infisical.com](https://app.infisical.com)\n    61→2. Create a `/global/` folder and add the required build secrets listed in `src/package/.env.example`\n    62→3. Create `/branches/<branch>/` folders as needed for per-branch deployment secrets\n    63→4. Create a machine identity with Universal Auth and add `INFISICAL_CLIENT_ID`, `INFISICAL_CLIENT_SECRET`, `INFISICAL_PROJECT_ID`, and `INFISICAL_ENVIRONMENT` to your `.env`\n    64→\n    65→### GitHub token\n    66→\n    67→Create a token at github.com/settings/tokens and enable the **`repo`** scope (the top-level checkbox). This gives the agent the read/write access it needs to clone your repo and push commits back. Add it to the Infisical `/global/` folder as `GITHUB_TOKEN`.\n    68→\n    69→### Branch setup\n    70→\n    71→The agent will not run when checked out on `main` — this is intentional to prevent accidental pushes to the main branch. Before running the agent, make sure you're on a feature branch:\n    72→\n    73→```bash\n    74→git checkout -b feature/my-app\n    75→git push -u origin feature/my-app\n    76→```\n    77→\n    78→The agent uses your current branch by default. Use `--branch` to override and target a different branch than the one you have checked out locally.\n    79→\n    80→---\n    81→\n    82→## Running Locally\n    83→\n    84→Runs the agent in a Docker container on your machine.\n    85→\n    86→### Additional prerequisites\n    87→\n    88→- **Docker Desktop** — must be installed and running. Download at docker.com/products/docker-desktop.\n    89→\n    90→### Usage\n    91→\n    92→```bash\n    93→npm run agent -- -p \"<prompt>\"\n    94→npm run agent -- --branch feature/my-app -p \"<prompt>\"\n    95→npm run agent -- --branch feature/my-app --push-branch feature/xyz -p \"<prompt>\"\n    96→```\n    97→\n    98→Starts a container, queues the prompt, then detaches. The container clones your repo, processes the prompt, commits and pushes results, then exits.\n    99→\n   100→Use `--push-branch` if you want to clone from one branch but push results to a different one.\n   101→\n   102→### Interactive mode\n   103→\n   104→```bash\n   105→npm run agent -- -i\n   106→```\n   107→\n   108→Chat with the agent in real time. Output is streamed via event polling. Press ESC to interrupt the current message. On exit, the container detaches and finishes any remaining work.\n   109→\n   110→---\n   111→\n   112→## Running Remotely (Fly.io)\n   113→\n   114→Runs the agent on a Fly.io machine instead of your local Docker. Useful for longer builds or running without tying up your machine.\n   115→\n   116→### Additional prerequisites\n   117→\n   118→- A **Fly.io account** at fly.io\n   119→- The **`flyctl` CLI** installed: `brew install flyctl` then `fly auth login`\n   120→\n   121→### Fly setup\n   122→\n   123→1. Create a Fly app to use as the container namespace:\n   124→   ```bash\n   125→   fly apps create app-building-agent\n   126→   ```\n   127→   Or create one via the Fly dashboard — the app name is just a namespace, no deployment needed.\n   128→\n   129→2. Add `FLY_API_TOKEN` (from fly.io/user/personal_access_tokens) and `FLY_APP_NAME` to your `.env`\n   130→\n   131→### Usage\n   132→\n   133→```bash\n   134→npm run agent -- --remote -p \"<prompt>\"\n   135→npm run agent -- --branch feature/my-app --remote -p \"<prompt>\"\n   136→```\n   137→\n   138→The agent provisions a `performance-cpu-16x` Fly machine (32GB RAM), clones your repo, builds the app, pushes results, and destroys the machine when done. Docker Desktop is not required.\n   139→\n   140→---\n   141→\n   142→## Monitoring & Control\n   143→\n   144→These commands work the same whether running locally or remotely.\n   145→\n   146→### Checking status\n   147→\n   148→```bash\n   149→npm run status\n   150→```"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_017QGnMaBu6sGT8UjWWXH9TK","session_id":"11df892d-2feb-4cba-a8ae-217c7a739ed7","uuid":"63666a35-a8ed-4f65-b8ce-deff830e8031"}
